{"id":"h019","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"For the indicator phenolphthalein (In^-),HInis colorless and In^- is red;K_dissis 10^-9. If one has a colorless solution of 50 ml phenolphthalein, how much 0.10 MNaOHsolution would one have to add to make itred: K_W = 10^-14 .\nIs the correct answer \"5.0 × 10^-5 liters\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The ratio [In⁻]/[HIn] equals 10⁻⁹/[H⁺] from the K_diss expression.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Taking a ratio of about 10 as 'red' gives [H⁺]=10⁻¹⁰ M, pH 10, and [OH⁻]=10⁻⁴ M.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Reaching [OH⁻]=10⁻⁴ M in 50 mL requires 5.0×10⁻⁶ mol OH⁻.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Adding 5.0×10⁻⁶ mol at 0.10 M NaOH requires 5.0×10⁻⁵ L, and the arithmetic checks out.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The indicator concentration is unspecified, so the mass balance is unclear and the needed OH⁻ depends on the total indicator present.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-0.4,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The calculation is valid only if the indicator amount is negligible relative to 5×10⁻⁶ mol, which is plausible but an assumption.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-0.2,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The threshold for 'red' is not stated, and a ratio of 10 is only a convention.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"A threshold ratio of 1 (pH 9) would give a different volume of about 5×10⁻⁶ L.","confidence":0.7,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.0996,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Under the standard reading, the answer 5.0×10⁻⁵ L is correct, though only as firm as its implicit conventions.","confidence":0.8,"lean":1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.3